==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Germanic|þarf-|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|terp-|lang=ang}}.  Germanic cognates include Old Frisian ''*thurva'', Old Saxon {{Latinx|''thurḅan''}} (Dutch ''[[durven]]''), Old Norse ''[[þurfa]]'' (Swedish ''[[tarva]]''), Old High German ''[[thurfan]]'' (German ''[[dürfen]]''), Gothic *[[𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌱𐌰]].  The Indo-European root also led to Greek ''[[τερπειν]]'', Old Slavic ''[[trěbě]]'' (Russian ''[[требоватъ]]'').

===Pronunciation===
{{IPA|/ˈðurvɑn/}}

===Verb===
'''þurfan'''

# to [[need]], to be [[obliged]] (to do something)

